Here’s a complete Node.js script that uses Puppeteer (which drives Chrome via the DevTools Protocol, not chromedriver) to:

1.  Open a “channel” (or “group”) page on Microsoft Stream (URL passed as the first argument).  
2.  Click the first video thumbnail it finds (i.e. the “most recent” video).  
3.  Listen for the HLS manifest (`*.m3u8`) request.  
4.  Hand that URL off to `ffmpeg` to grab the stream and save it as an .mp4 file.  

Save the snippet below as, say, `download_stream.js`:

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node

/**
 * download_stream.js
 *
 * Usage:
 *   node download_stream.js <STREAM_PAGE_URL>
 *
 * Prereqs:
 *   npm install puppeteer
 *   ffmpeg must be on your PATH
 */

const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');
const { spawn } = require('child_process');

(async () => {
  if (process.argv.length < 3) {
    console.error('Usage: node download_stream.js <STREAM_PAGE_URL>');
    process.exit(1);
  }
  const listUrl = process.argv[2];

  // Launch headful so you can authenticate if needed
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
    headless: false,
    defaultViewport: null,
    args: [ '--start-maximized' ]
  });
  const page = await browser.newPage();

  // Intercept network responses to catch the .m3u8 manifest URL
  let manifestUrl = null;
  page.on('response', resp => {
    const url = resp.url();
    if (url.endsWith('.m3u8') && !manifestUrl) {
      manifestUrl = url;
    }
  });

  // Go to the “channel” or “group” page
  await page.goto(listUrl, { waitUntil: 'networkidle2' });

  // If you need to sign in, do it now manually in the opened browser window,
  // then press ENTER in your terminal to continue
  console.log('If Stream is gated behind login, please sign in now, then press ENTER here...');
  await new Promise(resolve => process.stdin.once('data', () => resolve()));

  // Click the first video thumbnail on the page
  // (selector may need tweaking if MS changes their markup)
  const firstVideoLink = await page.$eval(
    'a[data-automation-id="video-tile-link"], a.ms-stream-video-thumbnail__link',
    a => a.href
  );
  console.log('→ Navigating to video page:', firstVideoLink);
  await page.goto(firstVideoLink, { waitUntil: 'networkidle2' });

  // Give the player a few seconds to fire off its manifest request
  await page.waitForTimeout(5000);

  if (!manifestUrl) {
    console.error('✖ Could not detect .m3u8 manifest URL.');
    await browser.close();
    process.exit(1);
  }
  console.log('✔ Found manifest URL:', manifestUrl);

  await browser.close();

  // Build an output filename (timestamped)
  const outName = `stream_backup_${Date.now()}.mp4`;

  console.log(`🡒 Spawning ffmpeg to download to "${outName}"…`);
  const ff = spawn('ffmpeg', [
    '-y',               // overwrite output
    '-i', manifestUrl,  // input is the HLS manifest
    '-c', 'copy',       // just remux, no re-encode
    outName
  ], { stdio: 'inherit' });

  ff.on('exit', code => {
    if (code === 0) {
      console.log(`✔ Download complete: ${outName}`);
    } else {
      console.error(`✖ ffmpeg exited with code ${code}`);
    }
  });
})();
```

How it works:

1.  Puppeteer launches a real Chrome instance (not via chromedriver).  
2.  You navigate to your Stream page; if authentication is required you can do it interactively.  
3.  The script clicks the first video tile it finds.  
4.  Puppeteer listens on all network responses for a URL ending in `.m3u8` (the HLS manifest).  
5.  Once it has that URL, it closes the browser and calls `ffmpeg -i <manifest> -c copy output.mp4`, which downloads and remuxes the HLS stream into a single MP4.  

Requirements:

- Node.js (tested on v14+)  
- npm install puppeteer  
- ffmpeg on your PATH (https://ffmpeg.org/)  

